WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.782
Now let's use regex in a more realistic cleaning task.

00:00:03.782 --> 00:00:08.442
Sometimes useful information is buried inside a longer text field.

00:00:08.442 --> 00:00:13.537
For example, a support note may include one or more email addresses.

00:00:13.537 --> 00:00:19.142
In this example, we have a support note that includes maria at example dot com and sales at

00:00:19.142 --> 00:00:21.667
shop dot org.

00:00:21.667 --> 00:00:28.467
The pattern looks for a sequence of word characters, dots, or dashes, followed by the at symbol,

00:00:28.467 --> 00:00:32.442
followed by a domain and a final dot extension.

00:00:32.442 --> 00:00:38.497
The function re dot findall returns all pieces of text that match the pattern.

00:00:38.497 --> 00:00:42.197
Run the cell and inspect the list of emails.

00:00:42.197 --> 00:00:45.577
A beginner-friendly rule is to start simple.

00:00:45.577 --> 00:00:48.367
Test your pattern on a few examples.

00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:53.182
Then add more cases only when you understand what the current pattern is doing.

00:00:53.182 --> 00:00:58.077
In real projects, email validation can become more complex.

00:00:58.077 --> 00:01:04.204
But for data extraction and cleaning, a simple tested pattern is often a useful starting point.
